## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Models real build/dependency graphs where costs accumulate; asks for both memoized and tabular DP to compare patterns and understand trade-offs. Emphasizes modulo arithmetic and edge-case handling for unreachable sinks and tight budgets.

## Key Concepts

- DP on DAG with an extra weight dimension
- Top-down memoization with (node, remainingBudget)
- Bottom-up DP in topological order with weight axis
- Modulo arithmetic and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized DFS
function countPaths(n, edges, W) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) adj[u].push([v, w]);
  const memo = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(W + 1).fill(-1));
  const dfs = (u, rem) => {
    if (u === n - 1) return 1;
    if (memo[u][rem] !== -1) return memo[u][rem];
    let res = 0;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) if (w <= rem) res = (res + dfs(v, rem - w)) % 1_000_000_007;
    return memo[u][rem] = res;
  };
  return dfs(0, W);
}
```

```javascript
// Bottom-up DP in topological order
function countPathsBottomUp(n, edges, W) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }
  // Kahn's algorithm for topo order
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const topo = [];
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift(); topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(W + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= W - w; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] !== 0) dp[v][t + w] = (dp[v][t + w] + dp[u][t]) % 1_000_000_007;
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= W; t++) ans = (ans + dp[n - 1][t]) % 1_000_000_007;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory if W is very large but reachable weights are sparse? 
- What changes if edges can be negative but the graph is still acyclic?

## Why This Is Asked

Quicksort with many duplicates can degrade; 3-way partitioning reduces swaps and comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- In-place iterative sorting (no recursion)
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid degeneracy
- Space: O(log n) for manual stack

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3WayIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [];
  stack.push([0, arr.length - 1]);
  while (stack.length) {
    const [l, r] = stack.pop();
    if (l >= r) continue;
    const m = l + Math.floor((r - l) / 2);
    // median-of-three to pick pivot
    if (arr[l] > arr[m]) [arr[l], arr[m]] = [arr[m], arr[l]];
    if (arr[l] > arr[r]) [arr[l], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[l]];
    if (arr[m] > arr[r]) [arr[m], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[m]];
    const pivot = arr[m];
    // Dutch National Flag partition
    let i = l, lt = l, gt = r;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    stack.push([l, lt - 1]);
    stack.push([gt + 1, r]);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem validates DP mastery (two implementations) and handling of substring lookups with a dictionary. It stresses practical constraints and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bottom-up DP, Memoization, Time: O(N * maxLen)
- Hash-map lookups for dictionary scores
- Edge cases: no decomposition, empty input, overlapping choices

## Code Example
```javascript
// Bottom-up
function maxScore(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const n = s.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(NEG);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(i, maxLen); len++) {
      const substr = s.slice(i - len, i);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr) && dp[i - len] !== NEG) {
        dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[i - len] + dict[substr]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === NEG ? -1 : dp[n];
}

// Top-down
function maxScoreTopDown(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const memo = new Map();
  function solve(i) {
    if (i === s.length) return 0;
    if (memo.has(i)) return memo.get(i);
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(maxLen, s.length - i); len++) {
      const substr = s.substring(i, i + len);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr)) {
        const val = dict[substr] + solve(i + len);
        if (val > best) best = val;
      }
    }
    memo.set(i, best);
    return best;
  }
  const res = solve(0);
  return res === -Infinity ? -1 : res;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt it if dictionary lookups are expensive and you need pruning?
- How would you extend to handle negative scores or required minimum total?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess understanding of Trie data structure and practical prefix search used in autocompletion; tests familiarity with basic data structures and iteration over strings.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with 26-way children
- insert/search/startsWith time complexity O(m)
- memory proportional to number of unique prefixes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.end=false; } }
class Trie { constructor(){ this.root = new TrieNode(); }
 insert(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) p.next[idx]=new TrieNode(); p=p.next[idx]; } p.end=true; }
 search(word){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of word){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return p.end; }
 startsWith(prefix){ let p=this.root; for(let ch of prefix){ let idx=ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if(!p.next[idx]) return false; p=p.next[idx]; } return true; }
}
```

